 :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121518} {:answer/id #uuid "eb2a4026-9a30-57e8-a31c-3c06788c36b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "not a critical point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121519} {:answer/id #uuid "207f2967-8496-5a9e-88f8-aba4ca44f2e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "saddle point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121520} {:answer/id #uuid "528656e1-de5b-5574-9c6d-9c343b8f9305" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "local maximum" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121521} {:answer/id #uuid "58e91dd6-8a5c-5dac-bf79-fcf5d7a52091" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "local minimum" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121522}] :answer-field/id #uuid "a2522a01-cce7-51e3-a966-267ce3351d43" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121517}] :question/id #uuid "e025078b-b821-5afd-8d42-a7c7c420b244" :question/math-academy-id "q-163721" :question/problem "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x = a ∈ R^{2}$ is given by\n$f(x) \\approx - 1 + \\frac{1}{2}(x - a)^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}(x - a)$.\n\nGiven that the eigenvalues of $f^{″}(a)$ are $λ_{1} = 3 - \\sqrt{5}$ and $λ_{2} = 3 + \\sqrt{5}$, what type of point is $a$?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124821 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124570 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "40d58ee6-1121-5ddc-a96f-1a7fa7a193dd" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Local Extrema Using Sylvester's Criterion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a4ceb8c-14f0-56c6-8c95-f0ee15b4651f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121524} {:answer/id #uuid "eac39722-5b4b-5e3f-abe3-0ec8369122a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121525} {:answer/id #uuid "f4428592-ed70-51da-961c-e1f39f88ff4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121526} {:answer/id #uuid "0f8e477a-ee9a-5e8a-a0d8-7609b5654512" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121527} {:answer/id #uuid "4cb59655-80cf-5782-9754-fdb5fa7a7b78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121528}] :answer-field/id #uuid "1d8caccd-460a-5673-8d6f-d66dff746801" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121523}] :question/id #uuid "b5496aa6-8f04-55cb-807e-38886a8c0e30" :question/math-academy-id "q-163722" :question/problem "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x = a ∈ R^{2}$ is given by\n$f(x) \\approx - 12 + \\frac{1}{2}(x - a)^{T}A(x - a)$,\n\nwhere\n\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $a_{11} =-2$ and $det(A) =-24$\n2. $A$ is positive-definite\n3. $a$ is a local minimum of $f$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433378 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124646 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ee9cbe8-ad40-5155-b42d-1802f36adf27" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Global Extrema of a Multivariable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c750366-87fd-5df1-9550-7377df9d1451" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,0,-1)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433354} {:answer/id #uuid "48052d29-4422-5f9a-b0b0-95c416d880f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,2,-2.5)$ and $(1,0,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433357} {:answer/id #uuid "981d803b-2b71-589c-9dd7-89b262f35507" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,0,-1)$ and $(2,-3,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433362} {:answer/id #uuid "c1664007-041b-58fe-aa78-e1585e47911c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,2,-2.5)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433365} {:answer/id #uuid "eedfc3db-8fe3-54f8-8907-da861cf5c513" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,2,-2.5)$ and $(-1,-2,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433371}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c1664007-041b-58fe-aa78-e1585e47911c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,2,-2.5)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433365} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e92c66f-883a-5be4-b481-34094dc1948a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433375}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5ccb6a2f-baf3-5789-838a-0e294ce49920" :question/math-academy-id "q-163794" :question/problem "![](images/f8/f82404522255a2e5b8be8b45d39062bd2239c15294024c02d6d4ad3dd3f44809.png)\n\nThe graph $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above. What are the global minimum points of the function?" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following definitions:\n\n- The global minimum of a function is the smallest value it attains on its domain. There can only be (at most) one global minimum.\n\n- A global minimum point is a point $(x,y,z)$ where a function reaches a global minimum. There can be zero, one, or many global minimum points.\n\nFor our function, the minimum value reached by the function is $f(x,y)=-2.5.$ Therefore, the global minimum is $-2.5.$ The function reaches this value when $(x,y)=(-1,2),$ so the global minimum point is $(-1,2,-2.5).$\n\n![](images/29/29d2b703b9c17aa69bb41076e4bf40f509af4b44bd3ade14dbdeaec7cad25120.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284436 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124646 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ee9cbe8-ad40-5155-b42d-1802f36adf27" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Global Extrema of a Multivariable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a606ca6-3907-55d4-bc5d-c66042100c2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281782} {:answer/id #uuid "3a753385-0a8a-5dfe-bed7-80c60cf215cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282204} {:answer/id #uuid "7feb410e-98b1-5206-95a7-24a5cc00115e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282789} {:answer/id #uuid "a69082ce-57b6-51d8-9d0d-481977368dc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283138} {:answer/id #uuid "c93a332b-7da6-520a-a57f-2512abf83643" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283448}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3a753385-0a8a-5dfe-bed7-80c60cf215cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282204} :answer-field/id #uuid "8db6d840-3b00-5182-9642-94796d437bc3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284177}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "999c6d8f-f6eb-52c5-981d-e4dac50005ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-163838" :question/problem "![](images/78/784f2cbc652df83239e40dbe830675a4c3ce99c9f51dec913ff2ec1f62612cdc.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above.\nWhat is the global maximum of the function?" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following definitions:\n\n- The global maximum of a function is the largest value it attains on its domain. There can only be (at most) one global maximum.\n\n- A global maximum point is a point $(x,y,z)$ where a function reaches a global maximum. There can be zero, one, or many global maximum points.\n\nFor our function, the maximum value reached by the function is $f(x,y)=4.$ Therefore, the global maximum is $4.$ The function reaches this value at $(x,y)=(-3,2).$\n\n![](images/39/39b694d4ea57e59d97102963009322aeef84d5fa58df07dbc854a73972ad7458.png)"}]}
